  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Solve for $d$ where $\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d}=1.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We note that $d=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3d$ and $4d.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3d,4d)=12d.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12d,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =1 \\\\ 12d(\\frac{10-d}{3d}+\\frac{3d-5}{4d}) & =12d\\cdot 1 \\\\ 12d\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{3d}+12d\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =12d \\\\ 4\\cdot 3d\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{3d}+3\\cdot 4d\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{4d} & =12d \\\\ 4\\cdot \\cancel{3d}\\cdot \\frac{10-d}{\\cancel{3d}}+3\\cdot \\cancel{4d}\\cdot \\frac{3d-5}{\\cancel{4d}} & =12d \\\\ 4(10-d)+3(3d-5) & =12d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $d,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(10-d)+3(3d-5) & =12d \\\\ 40-4d+9d-15 & =12d \\\\ 25+5d & =12d \\\\ -7d & =-25 \\\\ d & =\\frac{25}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $d=0$ as a solution. However, since $d=\\frac{25}{7}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $d=\\frac{25}{7}.$",
